1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,105
This is Canada,

2
00:00:01,105 --> 00:00:03,660
the second largest country on Earth.

3
00:00:04,010 --> 00:00:06,472
And yet it has roughly the same number

4
00:00:06,472 --> 00:00:08,870
of people as the state of California.

5
00:00:09,220 --> 00:00:09,910
In fact,

6
00:00:09,910 --> 00:00:13,669
Canada only passed forty million people in 2023.

7
00:00:13,669 --> 00:00:16,277
If the country were a huge house,

8
00:00:16,277 --> 00:00:19,960
almost everyone would be standing in one narrow

9
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:20,650
hallway.

10
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:22,102
Even stranger,

11
00:00:22,102 --> 00:00:25,188
most of those people live squeezed into a

12
00:00:25,188 --> 00:00:28,420
thin strip right next to the United States.

13
00:00:28,770 --> 00:00:31,331
So why does almost everyone in a giant

14
00:00:31,331 --> 00:00:33,630
country live along one edge of it?

15
00:00:36,580 --> 00:00:38,266
By the end of this video,

16
00:00:38,266 --> 00:00:39,887
you'll see how the cold,

17
00:00:39,887 --> 00:00:40,925
a rocky shield,

18
00:00:40,925 --> 00:00:43,714
two rivers and one railway drew this line,

19
00:00:43,714 --> 00:00:46,956
and why the United States keeps pulling Canadians

20
00:00:46,956 --> 00:00:47,410
south.

21
00:00:47,760 --> 00:00:51,133
Canada covers almost ten million square kilometers.

22
00:00:51,133 --> 00:00:53,986
That's bigger than the whole United States,

23
00:00:53,986 --> 00:00:56,516
and more than twenty times the size of

24
00:00:56,516 --> 00:00:56,970
Spain.

25
00:00:57,320 --> 00:00:59,924
It's so wide that it stretches across six

26
00:00:59,924 --> 00:01:00,668
time zones.

27
00:01:00,668 --> 00:01:03,086
When it's breakfast time in Vancouver,

28
00:01:03,086 --> 00:01:06,620
people in Newfoundland are already thinking about lunch.

29
00:01:06,970 --> 00:01:09,410
It also has more lakes than any other

30
00:01:09,410 --> 00:01:10,566
country on Earth,

31
00:01:10,566 --> 00:01:13,199
and one of the longest coastlines in the

32
00:01:13,199 --> 00:01:13,649
world.

33
00:01:13,649 --> 00:01:14,484
So on paper,

34
00:01:14,484 --> 00:01:16,860
there's plenty of room for everyone.

35
00:01:17,210 --> 00:01:18,502
But spread evenly,

36
00:01:18,502 --> 00:01:21,289
that would be only about four people for

37
00:01:21,289 --> 00:01:22,920
every square kilometer.

38
00:01:23,270 --> 00:01:24,154
In reality,

39
00:01:24,154 --> 00:01:26,733
they are not spread evenly at all.

40
00:01:26,733 --> 00:01:30,122
Roughly two out of every three Canadians live

41
00:01:30,122 --> 00:01:33,511
within about one hundred kilometers of the US

42
00:01:33,511 --> 00:01:34,100
border.

43
00:01:34,450 --> 00:01:36,839
Look at the biggest cities.

44
00:01:36,839 --> 00:01:37,778
Vancouver,

45
00:01:37,778 --> 00:01:38,546
Calgary,

46
00:01:38,546 --> 00:01:39,399
Winnipeg,

47
00:01:39,399 --> 00:01:40,167
Toronto,

48
00:01:40,167 --> 00:01:41,020
Montreal.

49
00:01:41,020 --> 00:01:43,580
All of them sit in the south.

50
00:01:43,930 --> 00:01:48,164
Toronto's metropolitan area has around seven million people.

51
00:01:48,164 --> 00:01:50,386
Montreal has over four million,

52
00:01:50,386 --> 00:01:52,468
and Vancouver close to three.

53
00:01:52,468 --> 00:01:55,453
Just these three cities hold about a third

54
00:01:55,453 --> 00:01:56,980
of the whole country.

55
00:01:57,330 --> 00:01:59,996
Toronto is actually much closer to New York

56
00:01:59,996 --> 00:02:01,874
and Detroit than to Vancouver.

57
00:02:01,874 --> 00:02:04,661
Many Canadian cities have more in common with

58
00:02:04,661 --> 00:02:07,569
their American neighbors to the south than with

59
00:02:07,569 --> 00:02:10,720
other Canadian cities thousands of kilometers away.

60
00:02:11,070 --> 00:02:12,762
And Canada isn't unique.

61
00:02:12,762 --> 00:02:15,266
Australians crowd onto their coasts,

62
00:02:15,266 --> 00:02:17,838
and most Russians live in the west of

63
00:02:17,838 --> 00:02:18,853
their country.

64
00:02:18,853 --> 00:02:21,560
Giant countries are often mostly empty.

65
00:02:21,910 --> 00:02:25,382
Meanwhile the northern territory of Nunavut is bigger

66
00:02:25,382 --> 00:02:26,218
than Mexico,

67
00:02:26,218 --> 00:02:29,240
and home to only around forty thousand people.

68
00:02:29,590 --> 00:02:31,047
From space at night,

69
00:02:31,047 --> 00:02:32,712
the pattern is obvious.

70
00:02:32,712 --> 00:02:35,626
Canada looks like a thin string of lights

71
00:02:35,626 --> 00:02:37,360
along its southern edge,

72
00:02:37,360 --> 00:02:39,927
with almost total darkness above it,

73
00:02:39,927 --> 00:02:42,980
stretching all the way to the Arctic Ocean.

74
00:02:45,930 --> 00:02:47,971
The first reason is simple.

75
00:02:47,971 --> 00:02:50,450
Most of Canada is extremely cold.

76
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:51,799
In January,

77
00:02:51,799 --> 00:02:55,380
Toronto averages a few degrees below zero.

78
00:02:55,380 --> 00:02:57,462
Yellowknife and Iqaluit,

79
00:02:57,462 --> 00:02:58,712
further north,

80
00:02:58,712 --> 00:03:01,460
sit at around minus twenty-five.

81
00:03:01,810 --> 00:03:03,269
In much of the north,

82
00:03:03,269 --> 00:03:05,921
snow can cover the ground for more than

83
00:03:05,921 --> 00:03:07,114
half of the year,

84
00:03:07,114 --> 00:03:08,175
and Hudson Bay,

85
00:03:08,175 --> 00:03:10,430
a sea bigger than many countries,

86
00:03:10,430 --> 00:03:12,220
freezes over every winter.

87
00:03:12,570 --> 00:03:15,689
Even the southern prairies have brutal winters.

88
00:03:15,689 --> 00:03:18,418
Winnipeg is one of the coldest big cities

89
00:03:18,418 --> 00:03:19,327
in the world,

90
00:03:19,327 --> 00:03:21,926
and locals sometimes call it Winterpeg.

91
00:03:21,926 --> 00:03:25,110
Now imagine a thousand kilometers further north.

92
00:03:25,460 --> 00:03:28,121
That leaves a very short growing season.

93
00:03:28,121 --> 00:03:29,289
In the far south,

94
00:03:29,289 --> 00:03:31,884
farmers have several frost free months.

95
00:03:31,884 --> 00:03:33,571
Across much of the north,

96
00:03:33,571 --> 00:03:35,388
there are only a few weeks,

97
00:03:35,388 --> 00:03:37,400
far too little for most crops.

98
00:03:37,750 --> 00:03:40,073
Under around half of the country,

99
00:03:40,073 --> 00:03:41,850
the ground is permafrost.

100
00:03:41,850 --> 00:03:43,627
It stays frozen all year,

101
00:03:43,627 --> 00:03:45,540
which makes building roads,

102
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:48,410
pipes and houses difficult and expensive.

103
00:03:48,760 --> 00:03:51,712
Further north you cross the tree line.

104
00:03:51,712 --> 00:03:52,545
Beyond it,

105
00:03:52,545 --> 00:03:55,951
the forest disappears and the tundra begins:

106
00:03:55,951 --> 00:03:56,859
low shrubs,

107
00:03:56,859 --> 00:03:57,995
moss and rock,

108
00:03:57,995 --> 00:04:00,190
frozen for most of the year.

109
00:04:00,540 --> 00:04:02,059
Then there's the rock.

110
00:04:02,059 --> 00:04:03,447
The Canadian Shield,

111
00:04:03,447 --> 00:04:06,089
a huge block of ancient rock covered by

112
00:04:06,089 --> 00:04:06,816
thin soil,

113
00:04:06,816 --> 00:04:08,005
lakes and forest,

114
00:04:08,005 --> 00:04:10,780
spreads across about half of the country.

115
00:04:11,130 --> 00:04:12,221
And in summer,

116
00:04:12,221 --> 00:04:14,912
much of the Shield turns into lakes,

117
00:04:14,912 --> 00:04:16,149
bogs and muskeg,

118
00:04:16,149 --> 00:04:18,549
soft wet ground full of insects.

119
00:04:18,549 --> 00:04:19,640
For centuries,

120
00:04:19,640 --> 00:04:22,258
the easiest way to cross it was not

121
00:04:22,258 --> 00:04:23,422
by land at all,

122
00:04:23,422 --> 00:04:24,440
but by canoe.

123
00:04:24,790 --> 00:04:27,184
Put the cold and the rock together,

124
00:04:27,184 --> 00:04:30,244
and only around five percent of Canada's land

125
00:04:30,244 --> 00:04:32,040
is good for growing crops.

126
00:04:32,390 --> 00:04:34,381
Now compare the west coast.

127
00:04:34,381 --> 00:04:38,504
Vancouver averages several degrees above zero in January,

128
00:04:38,504 --> 00:04:39,499
because warm,

129
00:04:39,499 --> 00:04:42,699
wet air from the Pacific softens the winter.

130
00:04:42,699 --> 00:04:46,040
The mountains behind it block the Arctic cold.

131
00:04:46,390 --> 00:04:49,957
And people have always lived where food can

132
00:04:49,957 --> 00:04:50,768
be grown.

133
00:04:50,768 --> 00:04:53,444
Which in Canada means one place:

134
00:04:53,444 --> 00:04:54,660
the far south.

135
00:04:57,610 --> 00:05:00,471
But cold and rock only explain half the

136
00:05:00,471 --> 00:05:00,972
story.

137
00:05:00,972 --> 00:05:02,760
The other half is water.

138
00:05:02,760 --> 00:05:03,905
Look at the St.

139
00:05:03,905 --> 00:05:06,480
Lawrence River and the Great Lakes.

140
00:05:06,830 --> 00:05:08,912
Long before Europeans arrived,

141
00:05:08,912 --> 00:05:12,269
Indigenous peoples had lived across this land for

142
00:05:12,269 --> 00:05:13,612
thousands of years,

143
00:05:13,612 --> 00:05:16,164
from the Pacific coast to the Arctic.

144
00:05:16,164 --> 00:05:19,857
And their settlements already followed the same logic:

145
00:05:19,857 --> 00:05:22,610
people gathered where food was reliable.

146
00:05:22,960 --> 00:05:24,685
On the Pacific coast,

147
00:05:24,685 --> 00:05:28,058
rich salmon runs supported large villages.

148
00:05:28,058 --> 00:05:29,940
Around the Great Lakes,

149
00:05:29,940 --> 00:05:32,372
the Haudenosaunee farmed corn,

150
00:05:32,372 --> 00:05:33,783
beans and squash,

151
00:05:33,783 --> 00:05:35,823
the famous three sisters.

152
00:05:35,823 --> 00:05:36,999
In the Arctic,

153
00:05:36,999 --> 00:05:38,881
the Inuit hunted seals,

154
00:05:38,881 --> 00:05:40,450
whales and caribou.

155
00:05:40,800 --> 00:05:43,029
The Great Lakes hold about a fifth of

156
00:05:43,029 --> 00:05:45,551
all the fresh surface water on the planet,

157
00:05:45,551 --> 00:05:48,014
and they are connected to the Atlantic by

158
00:05:48,014 --> 00:05:48,483
the St.

159
00:05:48,483 --> 00:05:49,070
Lawrence.

160
00:05:49,420 --> 00:05:51,983
When French explorers arrived,

161
00:05:51,983 --> 00:05:55,290
this river was a highway into the heart

162
00:05:55,290 --> 00:05:56,779
of the continent.

163
00:05:56,779 --> 00:05:59,507
Quebec City was founded in 1608,

164
00:05:59,507 --> 00:06:01,326
and Montreal in 1642,

165
00:06:01,326 --> 00:06:02,980
right on its banks.

166
00:06:03,330 --> 00:06:06,304
Montreal's location was no accident either.

167
00:06:06,304 --> 00:06:07,317
Just upstream,

168
00:06:07,317 --> 00:06:10,899
the Lachine Rapids stopped ships from going further,

169
00:06:10,899 --> 00:06:13,265
so goods had to be unloaded there.

170
00:06:13,265 --> 00:06:15,766
A city grew right at the point where

171
00:06:15,766 --> 00:06:17,320
the water route broke.

172
00:06:17,670 --> 00:06:20,249
The fur trade followed the water too.

173
00:06:20,249 --> 00:06:23,508
Beaver pelts were carried by canoe along rivers

174
00:06:23,508 --> 00:06:24,254
and lakes,

175
00:06:24,254 --> 00:06:26,630
and trading posts grew into towns.

176
00:06:26,980 --> 00:06:27,578
In 1670,

177
00:06:27,578 --> 00:06:30,899
the English king granted the Hudson's Bay Company

178
00:06:30,899 --> 00:06:34,020
control over all the land draining into Hudson

179
00:06:34,020 --> 00:06:34,352
Bay.

180
00:06:34,352 --> 00:06:37,075
It became one of the oldest companies in

181
00:06:37,075 --> 00:06:37,806
the world,

182
00:06:37,806 --> 00:06:40,662
and for two centuries it ruled a territory

183
00:06:40,662 --> 00:06:41,990
larger than Europe.

184
00:06:42,340 --> 00:06:45,043
But even that empire was mostly a network

185
00:06:45,043 --> 00:06:46,201
of trading posts,

186
00:06:46,201 --> 00:06:46,909
not towns.

187
00:06:46,909 --> 00:06:48,840
The furs came from the north,

188
00:06:48,840 --> 00:06:49,934
while the money,

189
00:06:49,934 --> 00:06:52,637
the ships and the people stayed along the

190
00:06:52,637 --> 00:06:53,860
coasts and rivers.

191
00:06:54,210 --> 00:06:56,371
Water also meant milder weather.

192
00:06:56,371 --> 00:06:59,187
Big lakes store heat in summer and release

193
00:06:59,187 --> 00:07:00,104
it in winter,

194
00:07:00,104 --> 00:07:02,592
so the land around them is warmer and

195
00:07:02,592 --> 00:07:04,360
greener than the interior.

196
00:07:04,710 --> 00:07:06,220
Water meant transport,

197
00:07:06,220 --> 00:07:09,108
trade and milder winters than the interior.

198
00:07:09,108 --> 00:07:11,471
So the first Canada grew as a chain

199
00:07:11,471 --> 00:07:12,981
of towns along the St.

200
00:07:12,981 --> 00:07:14,950
Lawrence and the Great Lakes.

201
00:07:15,300 --> 00:07:16,806
On the Atlantic coast,

202
00:07:16,806 --> 00:07:18,313
the story was similar.

203
00:07:18,313 --> 00:07:21,915
Fishing villages grew along the shores of Newfoundland

204
00:07:21,915 --> 00:07:23,028
and Nova Scotia,

205
00:07:23,028 --> 00:07:25,451
close to the rich cod grounds of the

206
00:07:25,451 --> 00:07:26,302
Grand Banks.

207
00:07:26,302 --> 00:07:28,791
People lived on the edge of the land,

208
00:07:28,791 --> 00:07:29,970
facing the water.

209
00:07:30,320 --> 00:07:33,198
That's also why French is still spoken along

210
00:07:33,198 --> 00:07:33,710
the St.

211
00:07:33,710 --> 00:07:34,733
Lawrence today.

212
00:07:34,733 --> 00:07:37,291
The colony of New France grew along the

213
00:07:37,291 --> 00:07:37,739
river,

214
00:07:37,739 --> 00:07:40,872
and the language stayed where the first settlers

215
00:07:40,872 --> 00:07:41,320
lived.

216
00:07:41,670 --> 00:07:42,523
Much later,

217
00:07:42,523 --> 00:07:43,163
in 1959,

218
00:07:43,163 --> 00:07:43,732
the St.

219
00:07:43,732 --> 00:07:45,439
Lawrence Seaway opened,

220
00:07:45,439 --> 00:07:48,354
allowing ocean ships to sail all the way

221
00:07:48,354 --> 00:07:49,919
into the Great Lakes,

222
00:07:49,919 --> 00:07:53,190
and turning these lakeside cities into ports.

223
00:07:53,540 --> 00:07:55,527
That pattern never went away.

224
00:07:55,527 --> 00:07:55,990
Today,

225
00:07:55,990 --> 00:07:58,705
around half of all Canadians live in the

226
00:07:58,705 --> 00:08:01,487
corridor between Quebec City and Windsor,

227
00:08:01,487 --> 00:08:03,010
along that same water.

228
00:08:03,360 --> 00:08:04,886
If you're enjoying this,

229
00:08:04,886 --> 00:08:05,557
subscribe.

230
00:08:05,557 --> 00:08:07,937
Every week we find the story hidden in

231
00:08:07,937 --> 00:08:08,730
another map.

232
00:08:11,680 --> 00:08:12,782
Further west,

233
00:08:12,782 --> 00:08:16,089
the border itself was drawn with a ruler.

234
00:08:16,089 --> 00:08:16,797
In 1818,

235
00:08:16,797 --> 00:08:20,261
Britain and the United States agreed on the

236
00:08:20,261 --> 00:08:21,994
forty-ninth parallel,

237
00:08:21,994 --> 00:08:24,907
and in 1846 the line was extended to

238
00:08:24,907 --> 00:08:25,930
the Pacific.

239
00:08:26,280 --> 00:08:27,556
Including Alaska,

240
00:08:27,556 --> 00:08:30,533
the Canada US border runs for almost nine

241
00:08:30,533 --> 00:08:32,021
thousand kilometers,

242
00:08:32,021 --> 00:08:35,424
the longest border between two countries in the

243
00:08:35,424 --> 00:08:35,920
world.

244
00:08:36,270 --> 00:08:38,496
But a line on a map doesn't make

245
00:08:38,496 --> 00:08:39,710
people move west.

246
00:08:39,710 --> 00:08:40,384
For that,

247
00:08:40,384 --> 00:08:42,070
Canada needed a railway.

248
00:08:42,420 --> 00:08:43,044
In 1871,

249
00:08:43,044 --> 00:08:46,230
British Columbia agreed to join Canada on one

250
00:08:46,230 --> 00:08:46,993
condition:

251
00:08:46,993 --> 00:08:49,487
a railway linking it to the rest of

252
00:08:49,487 --> 00:08:50,387
the country.

253
00:08:50,387 --> 00:08:51,219
Without it,

254
00:08:51,219 --> 00:08:54,406
the west might have drifted toward the United

255
00:08:54,406 --> 00:08:54,960
States.

256
00:08:55,310 --> 00:08:55,954
In 1885,

257
00:08:55,954 --> 00:08:59,102
the Canadian Pacific Railway was completed,

258
00:08:59,102 --> 00:09:01,893
linking Montreal to the Pacific coast.

259
00:09:01,893 --> 00:09:04,612
And it was built close to the border,

260
00:09:04,612 --> 00:09:08,190
partly to keep the western lands firmly Canadian.

261
00:09:08,540 --> 00:09:10,341
Building it was brutally hard.

262
00:09:10,341 --> 00:09:13,305
Crews blasted tunnels through the Rocky Mountains,

263
00:09:13,305 --> 00:09:15,978
and thousands of Chinese workers took on some

264
00:09:15,978 --> 00:09:19,290
of the most dangerous sections through British Columbia.

265
00:09:19,640 --> 00:09:20,741
On November 7,

266
00:09:20,741 --> 00:09:21,182
1885,

267
00:09:21,182 --> 00:09:24,412
the last spike was driven at Craigellachie,

268
00:09:24,412 --> 00:09:25,954
in British Columbia.

269
00:09:25,954 --> 00:09:27,422
For the first time,

270
00:09:27,422 --> 00:09:30,506
you could cross Canada by land from ocean

271
00:09:30,506 --> 00:09:31,240
to ocean.

272
00:09:31,590 --> 00:09:34,423
Towns grew wherever the trains stopped.

273
00:09:34,423 --> 00:09:35,131
Winnipeg,

274
00:09:35,131 --> 00:09:35,697
Regina,

275
00:09:35,697 --> 00:09:39,167
Calgary and Vancouver all boomed along the line,

276
00:09:39,167 --> 00:09:42,850
and they are still Canada's western capitals today.

277
00:09:43,200 --> 00:09:44,240
Decades later,

278
00:09:44,240 --> 00:09:47,705
the Trans-Canada Highway followed the same logic.

279
00:09:47,705 --> 00:09:50,547
It runs across the south of the country,

280
00:09:50,547 --> 00:09:53,666
linking the same chain of cities the railway

281
00:09:53,666 --> 00:09:54,290
created.

282
00:09:54,640 --> 00:09:56,124
To fill the prairies,

283
00:09:56,124 --> 00:09:59,160
the government offered land almost for free.

284
00:09:59,160 --> 00:10:01,724
Under the Dominion Lands Act of 1872,

285
00:10:01,724 --> 00:10:04,827
a settler could claim about one hundred sixty

286
00:10:04,827 --> 00:10:07,593
acres for a registration fee of just ten

287
00:10:07,593 --> 00:10:08,200
dollars.

288
00:10:08,550 --> 00:10:11,018
Hundreds of thousands of settlers came,

289
00:10:11,018 --> 00:10:12,129
many from Europe.

290
00:10:12,129 --> 00:10:14,782
The prairies became one of the great wheat

291
00:10:14,782 --> 00:10:16,139
regions of the world.

292
00:10:16,139 --> 00:10:16,818
But again,

293
00:10:16,818 --> 00:10:18,730
the farms stayed in the south,

294
00:10:18,730 --> 00:10:21,260
where the growing season is long enough.

295
00:10:21,610 --> 00:10:23,667
The north had its own boom.

296
00:10:23,667 --> 00:10:24,328
In 1896,

297
00:10:24,328 --> 00:10:26,678
gold was found in the Klondike,

298
00:10:26,678 --> 00:10:27,707
in the Yukon.

299
00:10:27,707 --> 00:10:30,792
Tens of thousands of people rushed north,

300
00:10:30,792 --> 00:10:33,436
and Dawson City grew into a town of

301
00:10:33,436 --> 00:10:35,860
many thousands almost overnight.

302
00:10:36,210 --> 00:10:38,635
Within a few years the gold ran low

303
00:10:38,635 --> 00:10:40,117
and most people left.

304
00:10:40,117 --> 00:10:42,543
Today Dawson City is home to only a

305
00:10:42,543 --> 00:10:44,362
couple of thousand people.

306
00:10:44,362 --> 00:10:45,305
In the north,

307
00:10:45,305 --> 00:10:46,585
booms come and go.

308
00:10:46,585 --> 00:10:47,730
The south stays.

309
00:10:50,680 --> 00:10:52,555
And then there is the neighbor.

310
00:10:52,555 --> 00:10:55,132
The United States has more than eight times

311
00:10:55,132 --> 00:10:58,120
Canada's population and the biggest economy in the

312
00:10:58,120 --> 00:10:58,530
world.

313
00:10:58,880 --> 00:11:02,800
Around three quarters of everything Canada exports goes

314
00:11:02,800 --> 00:11:04,340
to the United States.

315
00:11:04,690 --> 00:11:05,364
Highways,

316
00:11:05,364 --> 00:11:06,039
railways,

317
00:11:06,039 --> 00:11:09,073
bridges and pipelines all run north to south

318
00:11:09,073 --> 00:11:10,354
across the border,

319
00:11:10,354 --> 00:11:12,849
and the cities closest to it are the

320
00:11:12,849 --> 00:11:14,670
ones best placed to trade.

321
00:11:15,020 --> 00:11:18,793
The Ambassador Bridge between Windsor and Detroit carries

322
00:11:18,793 --> 00:11:22,045
more trade than almost any other crossing between

323
00:11:22,045 --> 00:11:23,281
the two countries,

324
00:11:23,281 --> 00:11:25,883
and Toronto's Highway 401 is one of the

325
00:11:25,883 --> 00:11:28,160
busiest highways in North America.

326
00:11:28,510 --> 00:11:29,843
Every new business,

327
00:11:29,843 --> 00:11:32,910
factory or warehouse chooses the same places,

328
00:11:32,910 --> 00:11:35,310
because that's where the customers,

329
00:11:35,310 --> 00:11:38,177
the workers and the transport already are.

330
00:11:38,177 --> 00:11:40,110
Growth attracts more growth.

331
00:11:40,460 --> 00:11:42,905
Immigration reinforces the pattern.

332
00:11:42,905 --> 00:11:46,300
Most of Canada's population growth now comes from

333
00:11:46,300 --> 00:11:47,047
newcomers,

334
00:11:47,047 --> 00:11:49,424
and most of them settle in the big

335
00:11:49,424 --> 00:11:50,578
southern cities,

336
00:11:50,578 --> 00:11:53,430
where jobs and communities already exist.

337
00:11:53,780 --> 00:11:56,845
Notice that the biggest cities also sit on

338
00:11:56,845 --> 00:11:57,343
water.

339
00:11:57,343 --> 00:11:59,125
Toronto on Lake Ontario,

340
00:11:59,125 --> 00:12:01,477
Montreal on an island in the St.

341
00:12:01,477 --> 00:12:02,190
Lawrence,

342
00:12:02,190 --> 00:12:04,043
Vancouver on the Pacific.

343
00:12:04,043 --> 00:12:04,898
The border,

344
00:12:04,898 --> 00:12:07,535
the water and the warmth all line up

345
00:12:07,535 --> 00:12:08,960
in the same places.

346
00:12:09,310 --> 00:12:10,653
Here's a fun fact.

347
00:12:10,653 --> 00:12:12,561
Canada's southernmost tip,

348
00:12:12,561 --> 00:12:14,752
near Point Pelee in Lake Erie,

349
00:12:14,752 --> 00:12:17,932
is further south than the northern border of

350
00:12:17,932 --> 00:12:18,780
California.

351
00:12:19,130 --> 00:12:21,521
The north isn't completely empty,

352
00:12:21,521 --> 00:12:22,084
though.

353
00:12:22,084 --> 00:12:22,577
Mines,

354
00:12:22,577 --> 00:12:25,320
oil sands and small towns exist there.

355
00:12:25,320 --> 00:12:27,852
But they are islands of people in a

356
00:12:27,852 --> 00:12:28,274
vast,

357
00:12:28,274 --> 00:12:29,540
frozen landscape.

358
00:12:32,490 --> 00:12:34,479
So what is life like on the other

359
00:12:34,479 --> 00:12:35,532
side of the line?

360
00:12:35,532 --> 00:12:37,813
The Inuit have lived in the Arctic for

361
00:12:37,813 --> 00:12:39,275
around a thousand years,

362
00:12:39,275 --> 00:12:41,790
and people before them for thousands more.

363
00:12:42,140 --> 00:12:42,868
In 1999,

364
00:12:42,868 --> 00:12:45,455
Canada created a new territory,

365
00:12:45,455 --> 00:12:46,182
Nunavut,

366
00:12:46,182 --> 00:12:49,012
which means our land in Inuktitut.

367
00:12:49,012 --> 00:12:50,063
Its capital,

368
00:12:50,063 --> 00:12:50,791
Iqaluit,

369
00:12:50,791 --> 00:12:54,510
is home to only around seven thousand people.

370
00:12:54,860 --> 00:12:58,159
Even the name Iqaluit tells you how people

371
00:12:58,159 --> 00:12:59,387
survived there.

372
00:12:59,387 --> 00:13:00,461
In Inuktitut,

373
00:13:00,461 --> 00:13:03,300
it means roughly place of many fish.

374
00:13:03,650 --> 00:13:06,634
Most communities in Nunavut can't be reached by

375
00:13:06,634 --> 00:13:07,007
road.

376
00:13:07,007 --> 00:13:09,805
There are no highways connecting them to the

377
00:13:09,805 --> 00:13:10,240
south,

378
00:13:10,240 --> 00:13:11,670
or even to each other.

379
00:13:12,020 --> 00:13:13,166
In some places,

380
00:13:13,166 --> 00:13:15,171
roads only exist in winter.

381
00:13:15,171 --> 00:13:18,321
Ice roads are built across frozen lakes and

382
00:13:18,321 --> 00:13:18,894
muskeg,

383
00:13:18,894 --> 00:13:22,188
and trucks carry supplies to remote mines for

384
00:13:22,188 --> 00:13:24,909
just a few weeks before the ice melts

385
00:13:24,909 --> 00:13:25,410
again.

386
00:13:25,760 --> 00:13:28,448
Food and fuel arrive by plane all year,

387
00:13:28,448 --> 00:13:30,934
and by ship during the short summer,

388
00:13:30,934 --> 00:13:33,689
when sea ice melts enough for the annual

389
00:13:33,689 --> 00:13:35,570
sealift to reach the coast.

390
00:13:35,920 --> 00:13:38,955
That makes everyday things far more expensive.

391
00:13:38,955 --> 00:13:41,473
A basket of groceries in the far north

392
00:13:41,473 --> 00:13:43,798
can cost two or three times what it

393
00:13:43,798 --> 00:13:44,960
costs in Toronto.

394
00:13:45,310 --> 00:13:47,292
Building is a challenge too.

395
00:13:47,292 --> 00:13:49,958
Houses often stand on stilts or piles,

396
00:13:49,958 --> 00:13:53,034
so their heat doesn't melt the frozen ground

397
00:13:53,034 --> 00:13:55,290
beneath them and make them sink.

398
00:13:55,640 --> 00:13:59,093
And yet the north matters enormously to Canada.

399
00:13:59,093 --> 00:14:00,387
It holds diamond,

400
00:14:00,387 --> 00:14:02,545
gold and other mineral mines,

401
00:14:02,545 --> 00:14:04,631
huge amounts of fresh water,

402
00:14:04,631 --> 00:14:07,580
and Canada's claim to the Arctic itself.

403
00:14:10,530 --> 00:14:12,036
Could this map change?

404
00:14:12,036 --> 00:14:15,179
The Arctic is warming several times faster than

405
00:14:15,179 --> 00:14:16,750
the rest of the planet.

406
00:14:17,100 --> 00:14:19,002
As the sea ice shrinks,

407
00:14:19,002 --> 00:14:23,518
the Northwest Passage through Canada's Arctic islands is

408
00:14:23,518 --> 00:14:25,816
open for longer each summer.

409
00:14:25,816 --> 00:14:29,778
The explorer Roald Amundsen needed three years to

410
00:14:29,778 --> 00:14:31,125
sail through it,

411
00:14:31,125 --> 00:14:32,630
from 1903 to 1906.

412
00:14:32,980 --> 00:14:34,924
The Arctic islands are huge.

413
00:14:34,924 --> 00:14:37,538
Baffin Island alone is one of the five

414
00:14:37,538 --> 00:14:39,280
largest islands on Earth,

415
00:14:39,280 --> 00:14:40,554
bigger than Spain,

416
00:14:40,554 --> 00:14:43,704
yet it's home to only around thirteen thousand

417
00:14:43,704 --> 00:14:44,240
people.

418
00:14:44,590 --> 00:14:48,084
A warmer north could bring longer growing seasons,

419
00:14:48,084 --> 00:14:50,482
new shipping routes and new mines.

420
00:14:50,482 --> 00:14:53,359
But melting permafrost also cracks roads,

421
00:14:53,359 --> 00:14:56,922
runways and buildings that were designed for frozen

422
00:14:56,922 --> 00:14:57,470
ground.

423
00:14:57,820 --> 00:15:00,631
Some scientists expect parts of the north to

424
00:15:00,631 --> 00:15:03,629
become better for farming as the climate warms.

425
00:15:03,629 --> 00:15:06,252
But good soil takes thousands of years to

426
00:15:06,252 --> 00:15:06,627
form,

427
00:15:06,627 --> 00:15:08,875
and much of the north is still bare

428
00:15:08,875 --> 00:15:09,250
rock.

429
00:15:09,600 --> 00:15:11,800
So even if the climate changes,

430
00:15:11,800 --> 00:15:15,581
moving millions of people north would take generations

431
00:15:15,581 --> 00:15:16,544
of new roads,

432
00:15:16,544 --> 00:15:17,781
homes and cities.

433
00:15:17,781 --> 00:15:18,400
For now,

434
00:15:18,400 --> 00:15:20,600
the line is not going anywhere.

435
00:15:20,950 --> 00:15:23,160
So let's put the whole story together.

436
00:15:23,510 --> 00:15:24,855
The north is cold,

437
00:15:24,855 --> 00:15:26,128
frozen and rocky.

438
00:15:26,128 --> 00:15:29,101
The best soil and the mildest climate are

439
00:15:29,101 --> 00:15:30,375
in the far south.

440
00:15:30,375 --> 00:15:33,347
And the rivers and lakes that carried the

441
00:15:33,347 --> 00:15:36,390
first settlers run right along the border.

442
00:15:36,740 --> 00:15:39,111
Then the railway that built the west was

443
00:15:39,111 --> 00:15:41,077
placed right next to that border.

444
00:15:41,077 --> 00:15:43,564
And the largest economy on Earth sits just

445
00:15:43,564 --> 00:15:45,010
on the other side of it.

446
00:15:45,360 --> 00:15:48,391
That's why most Canadians live near the US

447
00:15:48,391 --> 00:15:48,954
border.

448
00:15:48,954 --> 00:15:50,998
Not because of one decision,

449
00:15:50,998 --> 00:15:52,478
but because climate,

450
00:15:52,478 --> 00:15:55,720
geography and history all pointed to the same

451
00:15:55,720 --> 00:15:57,130
thin strip of land.

452
00:15:57,480 --> 00:16:01,766
Every map hides a story like this one.

453
00:16:01,766 --> 00:16:05,943
If you want to discover the next one,

454
00:16:05,943 --> 00:16:09,130
subscribe to Timeline Earth.
